[[!img media/nixos-lores.png class="noFancy" style="float: right"]]

# motivation

**libnix can be added to any package manager (PM) so consider this also a direction PMs will be heading in general with libnix.**

this post looks into the cargo+libnix impact, i.e. using the prototype discussed in the previous post [cargo-libnix](https://lastlog.de/blog/libnix_cargo-libnix_release.html), and we use:

* nixpkgs revision [1c3d5a53f03f2eb5677f6f3b34f0ef31261ba485](https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/commit/1c3d5a53f03f2eb5677f6f3b34f0ef31261ba485), from **Sat Dec 13 13:17:29 2025 +0000**
* see full details here: [nixpkgs-datamining](https://github.com/qknight/nixpkgs-datamining)

# rust & crates.io

a rust project usually uses crates.io dependencies like serde, syn and similar. these can be visualized with [cargo-depgraph](https://sr.ht/~jplatte/cargo-depgraph/):

[[!img posts/libnix/cargo-depgraph.png class="noFancy" style="width: 920px;"]]

these rust libraries (rlibs) are statically compiled into the final binary but during build time they are seperate entities. especially, since `cargo-libnix` shares crates.io dependency builds beyond the ./target/ folder, we can check what the speedup would look like, when it would be used for building software in `buildRustPackage`.

## `cargo` revisions in nixpkgs

[cargo in nixmultiverse](https://nixmultiverse.com/?pkg=cargo) lists 10 to 15 revision bumps per year from 2018 to 2026 but `cargo` is only one gear in a huge set of dependencies, we need 
 to study a better metric: `buildRustPackage` implementation changes!

